A. Cosmological data

In order to test its power on the Hubble tension we perform a MCMC analysis with Cobaya [75]. We consider a baseline dataset which consists in

• CMB data from Planck : low and high ℓtemperature and polarization, with CMB lensing [76];

• DES Y1 : weak lensing and galaxy correlations (3x2pt) [77];

• supernovae (SN) data from the Pantheon sample [78].

BAO data are then combined to this baseline in different ways. First, we split BAO measurements into a low-z (noted z < 1 hereafter) and high-z (noted z > 1 hereafter) datasets.

The low-z consists in

16

• 6dF : a distance measurement at zeff = 0.106 [79];

• SDSS DR7 [main galaxy sample (MGS)] : Rang and DH ≡1/H at zeff = 0.15 [80];

• SDSS DR12 : Rang, DH and redshift space distorsions (RSD) at zeff = 0.38 and zeff = 0.50 [81];

• luminous red galaxies (LRG) of SDSS DR16 : Rang, DH and RSD at zeff = 0.698 [82],

The high-z dataset consists in

• emission line galaxies (ELG) of SDSS DR16 : Rang, DH and RSD at zeff = 0.85 [82];

• quasistellar objects (QSO) of SDSS DR16 : Rang, DH and RSD at zeff = 1.48;

• Lyman-α (Lyα) absorption lines of SDSS DR16 : Rang, DH at zeff = 2.33;

• Lyα-QSO cross-correlations in SDSS DR16 : Rang, DH at zeff = 2.33;

Finally the local measurement of H0 from the recent SH0ES results [7] is combined with the baseline dataset and the chosen BAO data.

